      "problem": "$10{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=$",
      "worked_solution": "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3.$ So we have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}=\\frac{8}{125}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n10{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=10\\cdot \\frac{8}{125}=\\frac{80}{125}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{80}{125}=\\frac{16\\cdot 5}{25\\cdot 5}=\\frac{16}{25}\n$$",
